  "assessment": "The reply gives a plausible boundary-right rationale and recognizes the owner’s frustration (“that is fair to feel”). But it lets a broad, jurisdiction-dependent legal claim settle a moral question, and its peacekeeping advice (“that next time he will”) risks putting an unsupported promise in the man’s mouth. The conclusion is defensible, but the reasoning underplays neighborly cooperation and includes an avoidable honesty concern.",
